  <div class="prose">
    <h2>Why save totals are not a column</h2>
    <p>Save and shot totals stay on the linked source. Copying them into a spreadsheet froze at least one wrong pair.</p>
    <p>On Nov. 21, 2026 the <a href="https://api-web.nhle.com/v1/gamecenter/2026020200/boxscore">box-score feed for game 2026020200</a> (Flames at Oilers, Oct. 28) listed T. Jarry at 24 saves on 24 shots in 60:00, D. Cooley at 8 saves on 9 shots, and D. Wolf at 20 saves on 23 shots. That matches the notes already on the <a href="{{ '/nhl/starting-goalies/2026-10-28/' | url }}">Oct. 28 board</a>. The same day's check of <a href="https://api-web.nhle.com/v1/gamecenter/2026020220/boxscore">game 2026020220</a> (Oilers at Islanders, Oct. 31) listed T. Jarry at 18 saves on 18 shots and I. Sorokin at 21 saves on 24 shots, time on ice 59:26, in a 4-0 Oilers win. The board had said Sorokin made 20 saves on 23 shots. That note now quotes the feed, and it keeps the earlier figure so the change is visible. The <a href="{{ '/nhl/starting-goalies/2026-10-31/' | url }}">Oct. 31 board</a> is the place to read it.</p>
    <p>A preview, a recap, or a status report can be enough to confirm who started. It is a poor place to copy a save total from. The <a href="{{ '/articles/nhl-morning-skate-explained-starting-goalie/' | url }}">morning skate explainer</a> is where a projection stays expected until a team, the league, or the box score says otherwise.</p>
  </div>

